{"name":"Chameleon Tango","hex":"#c0c2a0","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"chameleon","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A small to mid-size reptile, of the family Chamaeleonidae, and one of the best known lizard families able to change color and project its long tongue.","A person with inconstant behavior; one able to quickly adjust to new circumstances."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["That changes or modifies its color."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/chameleon"},{"term":"tango","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Alternative letter-case form of Tango of the ICAO/NATO radiotelephony alphabet."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A standard ballroom dance in 4/4 time; or a social dance, the Argentine tango.","A Spanish flamenco dance with different steps from the Argentine."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/tango"}],"entries":[{"title":"Chameleon","blocks":[{"text":"Chameleons are a distinctive and highly specialized clade of Old World lizards with 200 species described as of June 2015. The members of this family are best known for their distinct range of colours, being capable of colour-shifting camouflage. The large number of species in the family exhibit considerable variability in their capacity to change colour. For some, it is more of a shift of brightness ; for others, a plethora of colour-combinations can be seen."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8a/Panther_Chameleon_738367_%28cropped%29.jpg/330px-Panther_Chameleon_738367_%28cropped%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chameleon","wikidata":"Q37686"},{"title":"Tango","blocks":[{"text":"Tango is a partner and social dance that originated in the 1880s along the Río de la Plata, the natural border between Argentina and Uruguay. The tango was born in the impoverished port areas of these countries from a combination of Argentine Milonga, Spanish-Cuban Habanera, and Uruguayan Candombe celebrations. It was frequently practiced in the brothels and bars of ports, where business owners employed bands to entertain their patrons. It then spread to the rest of the world. A number of variations of this dance currently exist around the world."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/42/Tango_dancers_in_Montevideo.png/330px-Tango_dancers_in_Montevideo.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tango","wikidata":"Q338450"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/e9/Verbreitung_von_Cham%C3%A4leons.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 1x","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/e9/Verbreitung_von_Cham%C3%A4leons.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AVerbreitung_von_Cham%C3%A4leons.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/39/Chameleons%27_Eye_Coordination_01.jpg/500px-Chameleons%27_Eye_Coordination_01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/39/Chameleons%27_Eye_Coordination_01.jpg/1280px-Chameleons%27_Eye_Coordination_01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/39/Chameleons%27_Eye_Coordination_01.jpg/500px-Chameleons%27_Eye_Coordination_01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Chameleons' eye coordination","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AChameleons'_Eye_Coordination_01.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a3/Ustad_Mansur_Chameleon.jpg/500px-Ustad_Mansur_Chameleon.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a3/Ustad_Mansur_Chameleon.jpg/1280px-Ustad_Mansur_Chameleon.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a3/Ustad_Mansur_Chameleon.jpg/500px-Ustad_Mansur_Chameleon.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Mughal era painting of a chameleon by Ustad Mansur","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AUstad_Mansur_Chameleon.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/43/Colour_change_and_iridophore_types_in_panther_chameleons.png/500px-Colour_change_and_iridophore_types_in_panther_chameleons.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/43/Colour_change_and_iridophore_types_in_panther_chameleons.png/500px-Colour_change_and_iridophore_types_in_panther_chameleons.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Colour change and iridophore types in panther chameleons: \n(a) Reversible colour of two males, one relaxed (m1), and one excited (m2). During excitation, background skin shifts from the baseline state (green) to yellow/orange, and both vertical bars and horizontal mid-body stripe shift from blue (m1) to whitish (m2).\n\n(b) Red dots show change in the CIE chromaticity chart of a male with green skin. white line: optical response in numerical simulations using a face-centred cubic lattice of guanine crystals with lattice parameter indicated with black arrows.\n(c) Haematoxylin and eosin staining of a cross-section of white skin showing the epidermis and the two thick layers of iridophores.\n(d) TEM images of guanine nanocrystals in S-iridophores in the excited state and three-dimensional model of an FCC lattice (shown in two orientations).\n(e) TEM image of guanine nanocrystals in D-iridophores.[18]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AColour_change_and_iridophore_types_in_panther_chameleons.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8c/Erp%C3%A9tologie_g%C3%A9n%C3%A9rale%2C_ou%2C_Histoire_naturelle_compl%C3%A8te_des_reptiles_%28Chamaeleo_chamaeleon_skeleton%29.jpg/500px-Erp%C3%A9tologie_g%C3%A9n%C3%A9rale%2C_ou%2C_Histoire_naturelle_compl%C3%A8te_des_reptiles_%28Chamaeleo_chamaeleon_skeleton%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8c/Erp%C3%A9tologie_g%C3%A9n%C3%A9rale%2C_ou%2C_Histoire_naturelle_compl%C3%A8te_des_reptiles_%28Chamaeleo_chamaeleon_skeleton%29.jpg/1280px-Erp%C3%A9tologie_g%C3%A9n%C3%A9rale%2C_ou%2C_Histoire_naturelle_compl%C3%A8te_des_reptiles_%28Chamaeleo_chamaeleon_skeleton%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8c/Erp%C3%A9tologie_g%C3%A9n%C3%A9rale%2C_ou%2C_Histoire_naturelle_compl%C3%A8te_des_reptiles_%28Chamaeleo_chamaeleon_skeleton%29.jpg/500px-Erp%C3%A9tologie_g%C3%A9n%C3%A9rale%2C_ou%2C_Histoire_naturelle_compl%C3%A8te_des_reptiles_%28Chamaeleo_chamaeleon_skeleton%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Skeleton of common chameleon","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AErp%C3%A9tologie_g%C3%A9n%C3%A9rale%2C_ou%2C_Histoire_naturelle_compl%C3%A8te_des_reptiles_(Chamaeleo_chamaeleon_skeleton).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ee/Nice_chameleon_portrait.jpg/500px-Nice_chameleon_portrait.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ee/Nice_chameleon_portrait.jpg/1280px-Nice_chameleon_portrait.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ee/Nic